<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Discovery activity: extends the sign-behavior work from Activity 05
     (factors of polynomials) to a polynomial divided by a polynomial.
     "Rational function" is not named today - framed instead as "a
     polynomial divided by a polynomial." Assumes the number-line sign
     diagram (mark each root, fix a sign in one interval, propagate by
     flipping at odd-multiplicity boundary points and keeping the sign
     at even ones) was already introduced live during today's Activity
     05 catch-up - see the note added to that file on 2026-09-06. This
     activity only has to add ONE new idea: a denominator factor creates
     a discontinuity (function undefined there) instead of a root
     (function equal to zero), but it goes on the same number line and
     obeys the same odd/even propagation rule.

     Timing: deliberately short. Class opens with roughly 20-25 minutes
     finishing Activity 05 (Sections 3-4, dropping the Section 4 bonus
     construction if catch-up runs long), leaving about 25-30 minutes
     for this activity in the 50-minute period.

     Desmos pages (share links, in order):
       Section 1: https://www.desmos.com/calculator/6t9ukhu4tg
                  "One Factor Rational"
                  f(x) = 1/(x-2)^n, slider n from 1 to 4.
       Section 2: https://www.desmos.com/calculator/rztlg4whww
                  "Two Factor Rational"
                  f(x) = (x+2)^m / (x-3)^n, independent sliders m, n
                  from 1 to 4.
       Section 3: reuses Activity 05's Section 3/4 Desmos page and QR
                  image (same share link and file:
                  assets/images/qr-desmos-sign-behavior-3.png) - it is
                  a general "type your own formula" page, already
                  starting from the placeholder f(x) = x^2, so no new
                  Desmos page or QR asset is needed.
                  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/5riqcszzk4

     Design notes:
     - Section 1 is a direct bridge from last activity: students
       already know the odd/even rule from Activity 05. The new
       idea is that a denominator factor's boundary point is a place
       where f is undefined (vertical asymptote), not a place where
       f = 0 - the sign still flips or holds by the exact same parity
       rule.
     - Section 2 deliberately keeps one factor on top and one on bottom,
       with independent exponents, to show that a factor's own parity -
       not whether it sits in the numerator or the denominator - is
       what controls its own boundary point, and that a root's behavior
       does not depend on what the denominator is doing (and vice
       versa).
     - Section 3's target, (x+2)(x-1)^2 / ((x+1)(x-3)^2), is a
       test of transfer: one odd and one even multiplicity in the
       numerator, one odd and one even multiplicity in the denominator,
       and no shared factors (no holes). Students
       must build the number-line diagram BY HAND - mark all four
       boundary points, fix a sign in one interval, and propagate - and
       commit to it BEFORE touching the Desmos page.
     - Kept light on writing on purpose. Sections 1-2 use shorter tables
       and fewer notice-questions than Activity 05's parallel sections -
       that time is meant to go to the number-line method during the
       Activity 05 catch-up, not to new writing here. -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-rational-sign-behavior">
  <title>Sign Behavior of Rational Expressions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      On the last activity, you found a rule for the sign of a polynomial near each
      of its roots, based on whether that root's factor has an odd or
      even exponent. Today we ask the same question when a polynomial
      is divided by another polynomial.
    </p>
    <p>
      A new kind of point shows up: a value of <m>x</m> where the
      denominator is <m>0</m>. The function is <em>undefined</em> there
      instead of equal to zero, but it still marks the edge of an
      interval on your number line, and the same odd/even rule tells
      you whether the sign changes there.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-one-factor-rational" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: One Factor, Now in the Denominator</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Open the first Desmos page below. It graphs
        <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{(x-2)^n}</m>, with a slider for <m>n</m>.
      </p>
      <image source="images/qr-desmos-rational-sign-behavior-1.png" width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
        <description>
          <p>QR code linking to the Desmos graph for Section 1.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
      <p>
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/6t9ukhu4tg"
             visual="desmos.com/calculator/6t9ukhu4tg"/>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-one-factor-rational-table" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Each number line below is already labeled with a value of
          <m>n</m>. For each one, mark <m>x=2</m> and label the sign of
          <m>f(x)</m> on each side.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x)=1/(x-2)^1$ to its right.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (4,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x)=\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^1}$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x)=1/(x-2)^2$ to its right.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (4,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x)=\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2}$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x)=1/(x-2)^3$ to its right.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (4,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x)=\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^3}$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x)=1/(x-2)^4$ to its right.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (4,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x)=\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^4}$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-one-factor-rational-bridge" workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At <m>x=2</m>, <m>f</m> is undefined instead of zero.
          There is no point on the graph there, it instead has an asymptote.
          Does the even/odd rule you found for roots of polynomials
          still work for this new formula at <m>x=2</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-two-factor-rational" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: One Root, One Discontinuity</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Open the second Desmos page below. It graphs
        <m>f(x) = \dfrac{(x+2)^m}{(x-3)^n}</m>, with separate sliders
        for <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>.
      </p>
      <image source="images/qr-desmos-rational-sign-behavior-2.png" width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
        <description>
          <p>QR code linking to the Desmos graph for Section 2.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
      <p>
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/rztlg4whww"
             visual="desmos.com/calculator/rztlg4whww"/>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-two-factor-rational-table" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try at least three combinations of <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>,
          including at least one even and one odd value for each. For
          each one, write your formula next to the number line, then
          mark <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=3</m> and label the sign of
          <m>f(x)</m> in each interval.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x) = $ to its right and blank space to write in the chosen formula.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (9,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x) = $};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x) = $ to its right and blank space to write in the chosen formula.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (9,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x) = $};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x) = $ to its right and blank space to write in the chosen formula.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (9,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x) = $};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <image width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, with the label $f(x) = $ to its right and blank space to write in the chosen formula.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (9,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x) = $};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>

      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-two-factor-rational-notice" workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does changing <m>n</m> ever affect whether the graph crosses
          or touches at <m>x=-2</m>? Does changing <m>m</m> ever affect
          whether the sign changes at the discontinuity <m>x=3</m>? What
          does that tell you about numerator and denominator factors?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-rational-challenge" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: Build the Diagram, Then Check</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Do <em>not</em> open Desmos yet. Consider
        <md>f(x) = \dfrac{(x+2)(x-1)^2}{(x+1)(x-3)^2}</md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-rational-challenge-diagram" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Mark all four boundary points on the number line below: the
          two roots and the two discontinuities. Determine the sign of
          <m>f(x)</m> in one interval, then use the odd/even rule to
          fill in every other interval. Label each interval with a
          sign, and mark each discontinuity clearly so it isn't
          mistaken for a root.
        </p>
        <image width="55%">
          <description>
            <p>A blank number line with arrows on both ends, for marking
            boundary points and signs by hand.</p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm]
              \path (-4,-0.8) rectangle (4,0.8);
              \draw[thick, &lt;-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
              \node[anchor=west] at (4.3,0) {$f(x)$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-rational-challenge-check" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          After your diagram above is complete, open the Desmos
          page below to check it. Replace the given formula
          with that needed for this problem
          <m>(x+2)(x-1)^2/((x+1)(x-3)^2)</m> and compare the graph to
          your prediction.
        </p>
        <image source="images/qr-desmos-sign-behavior-3.png" width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              QR code linking to the Desmos graph for Section 3 (shared
              with Activity 05).
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
        <p>
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/5riqcszzk4"
               visual="desmos.com/calculator/5riqcszzk4"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-rational-challenge-reflect" workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Where, if anywhere, did your diagram disagree with the graph?
          What did you fix?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
